WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 2 Leader Video
Unit 1, Lesson 2: Faith

1
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.430
So, several years ago, I was in a brand new apartment.

2
00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.390
I woke up in the middle of the night to do something.

3
00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.390
I don't remember what it was.

4
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:20.010
As I went back to bed, I rammed my toe so hard

5
00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.650
into the bed frame that I ended up breaking my toe.

6
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.510
I didn't know I broke it until I woke up the next morning.

7
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.010
It was black and blue. My whole foot was all messed up.

8
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.010
And I limped around for days. The doctor told me there was nothing that could be

9
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.770
done about it because I just simply broke my toe.

10
00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.310
The bed wasn't new, but the apartment was.

11
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.310
And when I woke up, it was pitch black.

12
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.110
I couldn't see a thing.

13
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.010
So naturally, I was so disoriented, didn't know where I was, didn't know what was

14
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:52.350
going on, that I forgot where the bed was because I forgot where I was.

15
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:57.790
Now see, growing up in my younger days as an early baby Christian,

16
00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.430
that's kind of how I thought of faith.

17
00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:05.770
I thought of faith as sort of this wandering in the darkness, trying to figure

18
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:10.910
something out, hoping that I don't stub my toe and hope I don't do something kind of

19
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:11.150
dumb.

20
00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:16.630
And in reality, in my life, I actually did stub my toe to the point where I walked

21
00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:22.130
away for quite some time. And as I came back to the church, I realized

22
00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:26.050
something that faith is not at all like wandering in the darkness.

23
00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:30.670
And what I've discovered is that so many students still kind of have that belief

24
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.830
that it's just either attaching themselves to what their parents believe and simply

25
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:39.270
just going along with something or literally wandering around the darkness hoping

26
00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.810
that what they believe is somehow true and that somehow in the end of all things,

27
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.450
when this life ends, that they'll find out that it is actually true.

28
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:54.630
What's interesting though, is that John says something in the very end of his gospel

29
00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.510
that starts to speak to us about really what faith actually is.

30
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.210
And it's in John chapter 20 verses 30 and 31.

31
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:08.230
And John says this, Jesus did many other miracles or miraculous signs in the

32
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.650
presence of his disciples, which are not recorded in this book.

33
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:17.690
But these are written that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son

34
00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:22.010
of God, and that by believing you may have life in his name.

35
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:27.490
What's interesting is what John says here is that these miraculous signs, all

36
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:33.370
of these things that John has recorded in his gospel are so that you might believe.

37
00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:39.110
We're given reasons. And what's fascinating about the Gospel of John is these seven

38
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.370
miraculous signs that John gives, starting with Jesus turning water into wine

39
00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:50.350
and then ending with this magnitude kind of a miracle with the raising of

40
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:55.610
Lazarus. And then if you want to include the eighth one of Jesus's own resurrection

41
00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:01.470
is that time after time, John sets it up so that we see increasing

42
00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:07.470
in measure all these signs that Jesus is exactly who he says he

43
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.090
is. And this is what we want students to actually get.

44
00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:16.050
That biblical faith is trust based on evidence.

45
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.950
And that translates really into a deeper relationship.

46
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.850
I mean, think about the relationships that you have.

47
00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.750
You don't just blindly trust somebody that you don't know.

48
00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:30.750
But the more you get to know somebody, the more you trust them.

49
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.590
The more you trust them, the deeper that relationship starts to go.

50
00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.990
And this is sort of how relationships work.

51
00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.450
It's no different than with our relationship with Christ.

52
00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:45.470
The more we know who He is, the more we know about what's going on and what the

53
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:51.150
gospel is, the more we can trust and the more reason we have to trust in what

54
00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.630
God is doing through Christ. And the more that we do that, the deeper our

55
00:03:55.630 --> 00:04:01.290
relationship with him goes. And this is exactly why we start

56
00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.130
in this lesson with this idea of your student's trust between friends.

57
00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:10.430
We want them to get that sense of what that looks like because our best and deepest

58
00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:14.970
relationships are built on a sense of trust, a deep sense of trust.

59
00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.070
And we have that because that person has either continually provided

60
00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.990
reasons to trust them, or maybe not reasons to trust them, right?

61
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.830
So we want our students to be able to see how that works.

62
00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:31.290
And what's really even more fascinating, I think, is when we start thinking about

63
00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:36.150
what faith is and how this works, we naturally kind of find ourselves in Hebrews 11,

64
00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:42.030
right? Hebrews 11:1. Now, faith is being sure of what we hope for and certain of

65
00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.350
what we do not see. This is what the ancients were commended for.

66
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:50.610
And then the writer of Hebrews goes through that huge wall of faith of all these

67
00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.670
people that had the right kind of faith.

68
00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:57.810
And we use this verse as sort of the foundational piece of what faith means, right?

69
00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.850
Being sure of what we hope for and certain of what we do not see.

70
00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:07.990
Well, that word certain, the word actually means or the idea it has

71
00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.030
with it, the idea of a foundation or substructure.

72
00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.830
So think of it this way. It's a basement.

73
00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:20.810
the idea that the writer is talking about is the basement in your home when you

74
00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.390
walk downstairs into your basement if you have one or if you've ever walked into a

75
00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:30.370
basement you generally don't fear that the whole house is going to cave in

76
00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.730
on you right because you've walked down to a basement has these huge metal beams and

77
00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:38.510
these metal poles and they're driven into the cement into the ground and the whole

78
00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:44.070
house is being held up by these beams and this basement and when you walk

79
00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.410
downstairs, you don't think anything of it.

80
00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:50.670
Why? Because the house is built on a solid foundation.

81
00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:56.490
And if foundation in a house is cracked, the house is starting to be no good and you

82
00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.790
can't really do anything in the house until you fix the foundation.

83
00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.870
So that's what the writer is saying.

84
00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:08.830
It's so sure, so fundamental that you can walk into a house and feel

85
00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.750
totally confident so much so that you don't even think about it.

86
00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.570
That's what faith is. It's that foundational piece of what we do not see.

87
00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:21.890
It's assurance of it. And in fact, if you take it one step deeper, the root word

88
00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:27.030
comes from the idea of an idea or one who stands

89
00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:30.930
immovable. It cannot be moved at all.

90
00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:36.790
One who is so rock solid and so dug into a foundation that it

91
00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.290
can't be moved. That is what our faith is.

92
00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.590
And that's what we want students to be able to see.

93
00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:43.450
I mean, think about it for a second.

94
00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:45.190
I was just thinking about this the other day.

95
00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.410
Why did Jesus keep his wounds?

96
00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:53.230
Why would Jesus, he's Jesus. He resurrects.

97
00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:58.510
And yet in the end of John's gospel, he shows Thomas his wounds.

98
00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:04.570
Thomas wants to see them. He wants to see for himself what it is that Jesus,

99
00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:09.850
why? It's Jesus. He's resurrected what more evidence do you need

100
00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:15.570
right but Jesus keeps his wounds and I've always found that really fascinating

101
00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:19.930
that of all things that Jesus could have done be like well I'm going to fix these

102
00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.290
wounds because why would I want to have these holes in my wrist and this gash in my

103
00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:30.050
side why would I want to keep those one more reason

104
00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:35.830
even if it was just for Thomas one more reason to show that this

105
00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:40.350
is who I am, and I've done exactly what I said I was going to do.

106
00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:48.230
So faith. We want our students to really understand the deep roots of faith,

107
00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:53.410
that it's not wandering in the darkness, but it's assurance and evidence of belief.

108
00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:58.490
So our small group time should be something that students can sort of take a step

109
00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:04.270
back and think about it and discuss with each other what it really means

110
00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.530
to have faith. And I want your students to start, and you should encourage your

111
00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:12.130
students to start thinking about, well, do I have my own faith?

112
00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:17.050
Or am I still just believing what my parents believed or what other people have told

113
00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:22.990
me to believe? Or am I starting to now believe things on my own based on what I'm

114
00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:28.630
now discovering? And that starts with asking your students have they considered

115
00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:34.550
the evidence? Have they considered all of the tools that are in here especially

116
00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:40.270
that God has given us to figure out is what Jesus

117
00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:46.050
did and what God is doing and the whole of salvation history, is that enough?

118
00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:52.610
So what we're talking about is finding a space where your students can start

119
00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:58.550
to make their faith their own it's easy for us as leaders to stand up in front of

120
00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:02.130
a bunch of students and tell them about our faith and read through Scripture and

121
00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:06.170
give them an interpretation of what our faith looks like and what faith they ought

122
00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:11.770
to have based on our faith but it's a whole different thing and the thing that we

123
00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:16.950
want you to start to encourage students so this transfer of what we're telling

124
00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.830
them to believe and what they believe based on the evidence that they're

125
00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:26.530
discovering on their own begins to happen is we want you to create a space where

126
00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:30.390
students can invest, they can pursue on their own.

127
00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:34.990
To go back to the idea of questions, they can ask the right questions or ask any

128
00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:40.850
questions for that matter so that the evidence that's here in Scripture will just

129
00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.190
come right off the page and into their lives.

130
00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.490
That's the kind of faith that sticks.

131
00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:48.610
That's the kind of faith that they'll take with them to college.

132
00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:50.890
That's the kind of faith that we want them to have.

133
00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:54.330
We don't want just them to accept what we're telling them.

134
00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:58.410
We want them to discover that what we're telling them is actually true.

135
00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.890
So, three things to keep in mind.

136
00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:05.910
One, drive home the fact that biblical faith is not without evidence.

137
00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:08.790
Biblical faith is not without evidence.

138
00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:14.710
Number two, that Jesus has taken significant time and energy to give us that

139
00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:20.550
evidence. And finally, number three, for students considering the evidence, it opens

140
00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:25.790
the door to a much deeper and more meaningful relationship with the creator of the

141
00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:26.070
world.

